The next step should be to complete the MCP → MOS resource/provenance/event path, because this closes an important gap in the architecture.

MOS MCP Integration v0.1 — Step 2

We now want this:
                         ┌──────────────────────┐
                         │      MOS Agent       │
                         └──────────┬───────────┘
                                    │
                              Attention/
                              Tool Selection
                                    │
                                    ▼
                         ┌──────────────────────┐
                         │    MOS MCP Gateway   │
                         └──────────┬───────────┘
                                    │
              ┌─────────────────────┼─────────────────────┐
              │                     │                     │
              ▼                     ▼                     ▼
        MCP Resources          MCP Tools             MCP Prompts
              │                     │                     │
              ▼                     ▼                     ▼
        Evidence/Context       Operation          Strategy/Input
              │                     │                     │
              └──────────────┬──────┴─────────────────────┘
                             ▼
                       PostgreSQL
                    authoritative state
                             │
                             ▼
                       MOS Event Bus
                       Redis Streams
                             │
              ┌──────────────┼──────────────┐
              ▼              ▼              ▼
            Cache        TerminusDB      Learning
         invalidation     projector      workers

This is a very important distinction:

Redis does not become the source of truth.

TerminusDB does not become the source of truth.

MCP does not become the source of truth.

PostgreSQL remains the authoritative MOS state.

1. MCP Resources

We should add a proper resource path.

For example an MCP server exposes:
resource://filesystem/project/readme
resource://github/repository/issues
resource://database/schema
resource://host/capabilities

MOS receives:
resource URI
resource contents
MCP server identity
capability identity
timestamp
content hash

and creates an evidence/provenance chain:

MCP Server
    │
    ▼
Resource
    │
    ▼
Evidence
    │
    ├── source
    ├── URI
    ├── content hash
    ├── captured_at
    └── provenance

That fits extremely well with the provenance model we've already established.

2. Do NOT immediately turn every resource into a Fact

This is important.

Suppose MCP returns:

"The PostgreSQL server is running version 17."

We should initially store:

Evidence
   ↓
candidate knowledge

rather than:

Evidence
   ↓
Fact

The cognitive pipeline decides whether it becomes knowledge.

So:

MCP Resource
      ↓
Evidence
      ↓
Interpretation
      ↓
Fact candidate
      ↓
Validation
      ↓
Fact Version
      ↓
Knowledge State

That preserves the distinction we've built into MOS between what was observed and what MOS believes to be knowledge.

3. Tool execution becomes an Operation

We already started this.

A tool call:

filesystem.read_file

should produce:

cognition.operations

with something conceptually like:

operation_type = mcp.tool_call
agent_id       = ...
started_at     = ...
completed_at   = ...
status         = completed

Then:

operation
    │
    ├── input → MCP capability
    │
    ├── input → arguments
    │
    ├── output → result
    │
    └── evidence → external information

This is exactly what we want for auditing.

4. Redis Streams becomes the event distribution layer

This is where I think the architecture gets substantially stronger.

Instead of having every component constantly query PostgreSQL:

PostgreSQL
 ↑    ↑    ↑    ↑
 │    │    │    │
cache graph worker learning

we use:

                 PostgreSQL
                     │
              authoritative commit
                     │
                     ▼
                MOS Event
                     │
                     ▼
              Redis Streams
                     │
       ┌─────────────┼─────────────┐
       ▼             ▼             ▼
     Cache        Graph          Learning
  invalidator    projector       worker

Redis becomes the fast distribution mechanism, not the database.

5. Example event

Suppose a new fact version is created.

PostgreSQL commits:

fact_version = FV-9281

Then MOS emits:

{
  "event_type": "fact.updated",
  "fact_id": "F-123",
  "fact_version_id": "FV-9281",
  "knowledge_state_id": "KS-42",
  "operation_id": "OP-991",
  "cache_keys": [
    "mos:fact:F-123",
    "mos:knowledge:F-123"
  ]
}

Redis:

mos:events

Consumers receive it.

Cache consumer

Deletes:

mos:fact:F-123
mos:knowledge:F-123
TerminusDB consumer

Updates the graph projection.

Embedding consumer

Re-embeds the new knowledge if required.

Learning consumer

Evaluates whether this changed a strategy or model.

This is much more scalable than making each subsystem tightly coupled.

6. MCP capability learning

This is another major piece.

Suppose MOS has three ways to obtain information:

Tool A
Tool B
Tool C

MOS can record:

Tool A
success: 98%
latency: 40ms
information value: 0.81


Tool B
success: 92%
latency: 600ms
information value: 0.93


Tool C
success: 99%
latency: 200ms
information value: 0.62

The MCP subsystem can then calculate a runtime capability score.

For example:

CapabilityScore =
    reliability
  + information value
  + latency efficiency
  + historical usefulness
  + context compatibility

But here's the critical MOS rule:

This score is NOT itself a Fact.

It's a runtime learning signal.

Eventually:

Observation
    ↓
Episode
    ↓
Learning analysis
    ↓
Strategy candidate
    ↓
Fact Version / Strategy Version
    ↓
Knowledge State

This keeps our immutable knowledge architecture clean.

7. This connects directly to your immutable facts design

The MCP system therefore works beautifully with the fact_versions / knowledge_states model we introduced.

Imagine MOS learns:

Tool X is usually the fastest way to inspect PostgreSQL schemas.

Initially:

Fact F1
Version V1


"Tool X is preferred for PostgreSQL schema inspection."

Later it discovers that Tool X became unreliable.

We do not overwrite V1.

Instead:

Fact F1
│
├── V1
│   └── valid until T2
│
└── V2
    └── current

And:

knowledge_state
    ↓
points to V2

TerminusDB can then represent the historical evolution as well.

That gives us:

What did MOS know?
        ↓
At what time?
        ↓
Why?
        ↓
From which evidence?
        ↓
Which operation produced it?
        ↓
Which agent learned it?
        ↓
Which version replaced it?

That's one of the strongest parts of the architecture we're building.

8. MCP Prompts

The third MCP primitive is prompts.

But we should not blindly treat MCP prompts as MOS knowledge.

Instead:

MCP Prompt
     ↓
Candidate cognitive procedure
     ↓
Evaluation
     ↓
Potential Strategy / Procedure

For example:

Prompt:
"Analyze PostgreSQL query performance"

could eventually become part of a MOS procedure:

Procedure:
PostgreSQL Performance Investigation


Steps:
1. inspect query
2. inspect execution plan
3. inspect indexes
4. inspect statistics
5. compare historical performance

Again, the MCP server supplied an input.

MOS owns the resulting knowledge.

9. The next database boundary

At this point I would introduce one more explicit concept:

mcp.capabilities

But not another giant generic table.

The purpose should be to persist the identity/version of discovered MCP capabilities.

Conceptually:

mcp.servers
mcp.capabilities
mcp.capability_versions

Something like:

mcp.servers
------------
server_id
name
transport
endpoint
trust_level
status
created_at

and:

mcp.capabilities
----------------
capability_id
server_id
kind
name
current_version_id
lifecycle

and:

mcp.capability_versions
-----------------------
capability_version_id
capability_id
schema_hash
description
input_schema
output_schema
discovered_at
supersedes_version_id

That is important because MCP capabilities can change.

We shouldn't cache:

tool_name = execute_sql

forever.

We need to know:

Tool
 ↓
Version
 ↓
Schema hash
 ↓
Current version

This also gives our Redis cache a natural invalidation key.

10. The resulting architecture

At this point the MOS stack becomes:

                 ┌──────────────────────┐
                 │       MOS Agent      │
                 └──────────┬───────────┘
                            │
                    Attention Gateway
                            │
             ┌──────────────┴──────────────┐
             │                             │
       Knowledge Retrieval            MCP Gateway
             │                             │
       ┌─────┴─────┐                ┌─────┼─────┐
       │           │                │     │     │
   PostgreSQL   TerminusDB       Tools Resources Prompts
       │           │                │     │     │
       └─────┬─────┘                └─────┼─────┘
             │                            │
             │                       Operations
             │                            │
             └──────────┬─────────────────┘
                        ▼
                  MOS Event Layer
                        │
                   Redis Streams
                        │
       ┌────────────────┼─────────────────┐
       ▼                ▼                 ▼
     Cache          Graph Projection    Learning
   invalidation                         workers
                                           │
                                           ▼
                                  Strategy/Knowledge
                                           │
                                           ▼
                                    PostgreSQL
                                    + TerminusDB

This is now becoming a genuinely coherent system rather than a collection of databases and agents.

The next concrete implementation

I recommend we now implement MCP Integration v0.1 — Part II in this exact order:

mcp.servers
mcp.capabilities
mcp.capability_versions
MCP resource discovery/read
MCP prompt discovery/get
PostgreSQL operation/provenance recording
Redis Streams event contract
cache invalidation worker
capability-performance learning
first MCP → Evidence → Knowledge candidate pipeline

Then we'll have a complete vertical slice:

discover → authorize → execute/read → record → cache → emit event → learn → eventually promote knowledge.

And importantly, this doesn't conflict with our PostgreSQL/TerminusDB architecture—it actually gives both databases a very clear role.
